# Agent Orchestration Safety (HARD RULE)

Even when asked for "max agents", "many parallel agents", or "24/7 parallel
work", DO NOT dispatch tasks that fight over the same resource. More agents must
never make the system worse. Use only as many agents as the work is genuinely
independent, not a target count. This rule wins over any "max agents" instruction.

## What "disjoint" means
Disjoint = no shared write target AND no shared upstream dependency being
modified. Different folders is not enough: two files that import the same type
being changed are NOT disjoint.

## Single-owner resources (ONE agent at a time)
| Resource | Rule | Forbidden |
|---|---|---|
| Dev server port | One owner per port | Second agent start/kill/rebind same port |
| Long-running process (API/DB/worker) | One owner | Duplicate start or `kill -9` without lock |
| Build output dir | One build agent | Parallel builds to same `dist/`/`build/`/`.next/` |
| Package install | One install agent per root | Parallel npm/pnpm/yarn/cargo/pub install |
| DB migrations | One migration agent | Parallel migrate/up/deploy on same database |
| Git destructive ops | One agent | Parallel rebase/clean/reset/force-push |
| Lock/mutex/PID files | Respect existing lock | Ignoring `.lock`, `flock`, PID files |
| Native iOS/Android sync | One `cap sync` + Xcode owner | Parallel edits to `ios/` + upload |
| Store/TestFlight upload | One owner per build number | Two agents bumping version or re-uploading |
| Production deploy | One owner per environment | Parallel deploys to same env |
| Shared config in a wave | One writer per file | Two agents patching the same config/router |

Read the repo's own scripts (`package.json`, project scripts) for the actual
ports and commands. Do not invent a competing dev/build command. Before starting
a server or build, check one isn't already running.

## Dispatch
1. Read-only agents (audit/grep/review): unlimited. Write agents: capped at 5–6
   per wave, and only as many as there are genuinely independent pieces.
2. Collision check before every dispatch: if a candidate needs a resource
   another agent holds, serialize or defer it. Never dispatch into a collision.
3. Priority: (1) restore working dev env, (2) fix compile/test failures,
   (3) rebuild, (4) feature/QA, (5) cosmetic/docs.
4. State the plan explicitly before starting, e.g. "5 agents on independent
   modules; 1 server owner; 1 build owner."
5. Freeze on breakage: stop spawning when a shared resource is broken; fix with
   one owner first, then resume.

## No agent grades its own work
- Writer ≠ auditor ≠ orchestrator. The agent that wrote a change does not
  approve it; the agent that planned the split does not audit its own plan. A
  separate agent reviews.
- Audit depth scales with risk: full review for auth, payments, native,
  migrations, or anything user-facing that can break; trivial/doc/read-only
  changes get a light check only.

## Responding to "max agents" / "asap" / "as fast as possible"
This is a standing instruction: when the operator says "max agents", "asap", or
"as fast as possible", interpret it as "reach a correct, verified result in the
least wall-clock time", NOT "spawn the largest number of agents". Then:
1. Decompose the task and find the real parallelism (independent, disjoint
   pieces). Run exactly that many write agents, up to the 5-6 cap. Unlimited
   read-only agents. Fewer agents is correct when the work does not split.
2. Assign single owners for every shared resource (each server port, build,
   install, migration, deploy) BEFORE dispatching. Never let "asap" put two
   agents on one port/build/DB.
3. If the dev env or a shared resource is currently broken, FIX THAT FIRST with
   one owner (restore server, dedupe stalled tasks) before spawning anything.
   "asap" never means pile more agents onto a broken base.
4. Each slice still passes the verification gate before it lands. Speed does not
   waive verification; unverified work is not "done", it's rework waiting.
5. State the plan in one line before starting, e.g. "asap: 4 write agents on
   independent modules, 1 server owner on the dev port, 1 build owner; E2E gate
   before any land."
"asap" changes HOW HARD you parallelize the independent work. It never relaxes
the single-owner, no-self-approval, or verified-before-landing rules.

## Only verified work lands; don't revert the trunk
- Build and verify each change in isolation (worktree/branch); it lands only
  after passing verification (builds, runs, no errors). A failing change is
  rejected and discarded, never merged then reverted.
- If broken state slips through, restore the last known-good state immediately,
  then fix the check that let it through.
- No full rebuild until compile/typecheck/tests pass OR a known-good backup
  exists. If a rebuild fails, restore the backup; never leave a half-written
  output dir. One rebuild owner per worktree.
- Never leave the user on a broken build. Fix or reject before cosmetic work.

## Failure response
1. Stop dispatching new server/build/install agents.
2. One agent: diagnose and restore last known-good state.
3. One agent: fix root cause (compile, config, deps).
4. One rebuild owner: rebuild/restart via project scripts.
5. Verify end-to-end, then resume parallel agents on disjoint scopes.

## Changing this rule
Do NOT edit, weaken, or override this rule without explicit operator approval.
If a task seems to require changing it, STOP and ask first, stating the change
and why. Repo-specific rules may ADD stricter constraints, never relax these.
